---
// Fall week 0: the welcome and bidding session, the first time the class
// meets. About thirty minutes of talk, then questions while bidding opens.
// Served at /decks/fall-week-0/. Year-specific facts (TAs, rooms, dates,
// the multidisciplinary project list) stay in announcements; the grade and
// the sessions are rendered from the handbook pages that own them.
import alexUlbrich from "../assets/decks/alex-ulbrich.jpg";
import kirstenWinters from "../assets/decks/kirsten-winters.jpg";
import GradeGrid from "../components/deck/GradeGrid.astro";
import Notes from "../components/deck/Notes.astro";
import PeopleSlide from "../components/deck/PeopleSlide.astro";
import QrSlide from "../components/deck/QrSlide.astro";
import Slide from "../components/deck/Slide.astro";
import StatementSlide from "../components/deck/StatementSlide.astro";
import TermSessions from "../components/deck/TermSessions.astro";
import TitleSlide from "../components/deck/TitleSlide.astro";
import { portal } from "../site";

export const title = "Welcome to Capstone";

const handbook = new URL("/", Astro.site).href;
---

<TitleSlide
  kicker="CS 461, fall week 0"
  title="Welcome to Capstone"
  subtitle="One team, one project partner, one project for the whole year"
  byline="Kirsten Winters and Alex Ulbrich, your instructors"
>
  <Notes time="0:30" cue="Wait for the room to settle, then introduce yourselves.">
    <p>
      Today covers what the course asks of you, how the grade works, and how
      you get onto a project. Bidding opens today and closes at the end of
      this week.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</TitleSlide>

<PeopleSlide
  kicker="The instructors"
  title="Ask us early"
  people={[
    { name: "Kirsten Winters", src: kirstenWinters },
    { name: "Alex Ulbrich", src: alexUlbrich },
  ]}
>
  <p>One address reaches both of us:</p>
  <p><strong>cs-46x-help@oregonstate.edu</strong></p>
  <p class="deck-small">
    Technical decisions, team dynamics, a project that's stuck: email, or
    knock on our doors.
  </p>
  <Notes time="1:30" cue="Each instructor says one sentence about what they help with.">
    <p>
      Office rooms change from year to year, so they're in announcements, not
      on this slide. The shared address goes to both instructors; use it
      rather than either personal email so nothing waits on one inbox.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</PeopleSlide>

<Slide kicker="What this course is" title="Not your usual class">
  <ul>
    <li>No lectures to sit through, and no exams</li>
    <li class="fragment">A real project for a real project partner</li>
    <li class="fragment">Working under uncertainty, as a team, every week</li>
    <li class="fragment">About nine hours a week in fall, meetings and class included</li>
    <li class="fragment">Start each assignment at least a week before it's due</li>
  </ul>
  <Notes time="2:00" cue="Pause on the nine hours; it surprises people.">
    <p>
      The nine hours is the figure the partner page gives: nine a week in fall
      and winter, six in spring, per student, meetings and class time
      included. Nothing in this course can be crammed into week 10, because
      the partner and your teammates are working on their own calendars.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<StatementSlide
  kicker="The top of the outcome ladder"
  source="Shipping guide, The Ladder"
>
  Shipped means people who aren't you depend on it.
  <Notes time="2:00" cue="Ask: who will run your software in spring?">
    <p>
      The Shipping guide measures shipped by one question: who ran the
      software? The rungs depend on the kind of project; the top always means
      people outside the team depend on it. Each team agrees with its partner
      which rung it's aiming at, and that agreed rung is the target. The
      slow clocks (maintainer review, app store review, IRB, partner IT) start
      in the first weeks, so read the Shipping guide this week.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</StatementSlide>

<Slide kicker="AI policy" title="Use AI tools, and own the result">
  <ul>
    <li><strong>You own the result:</strong> correctness, security, and licensing, whatever produced it</li>
    <li class="fragment"><strong>Delegate deliberately:</strong> say what you handed off and which checks made that reasonable</li>
    <li class="fragment"><strong>Respect the confidentiality boundary</strong> your team charter sets with your partner</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="deck-small after-list fragment">
    A weaker model or a tighter budget is no disadvantage: it means tighter
    checks and more validation of your own.
  </p>
  <Notes time="2:30" cue="Point at the third rule and mention NDA projects.">
    <p>
      These are the three standing rules on the Assignments Overview. What's
      graded is the fit between how far you delegated and how strong your
      checks are, never which tool you used. You can work with little or no
      AI if you prefer. What may go into which tool is set per project, in
      the team charter's AI and confidentiality section, and for an NDA
      project that section is written with the partner.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<StatementSlide kicker="Where to delegate" source="Assignments Overview, AI Policy">
  Delegate freely where your checks catch mistakes cheaply; validate yourself
  where a decision is hard to reverse.
  <Notes time="1:00">
    <p>
      Hard to reverse: architecture, database schema, authentication,
      deployment, data migrations, licensing. Cheap to check: anything your
      tests, CI, review, or a rollback path will catch. The Generative AI guide
      covers how to tell the two apart.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</StatementSlide>

<Slide kicker="Your fall grade" title="Four equal components" wide>
  <GradeGrid term="fall" />
  <Notes time="3:00" cue="Half the grade is individual: peer evaluation and individual evidence.">
    <p>
      This slide is drawn from the grade grid on the Assignments Overview, so
      it's always this year's numbers. Your project partner scores a quarter;
      your teammates score a quarter, for you alone; your own work is a
      quarter; your team's deliverables are the last quarter, where half of
      each sprint note is your individual contribution. The Bidding Survey
      counts: it's this week.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="Grades and your partner" title="The work runs through your project partner">
  <ul>
    <li>Your partner scores a quarter of your grade, twice a term</li>
    <li class="fragment">The assignments are there to support that work, not to compete with it</li>
    <li class="fragment">Know where your project stands every week, and say so early when it slips</li>
    <li class="fragment">Ask the instruction team as soon as something blocks you</li>
  </ul>
  <Notes time="1:30">
    <p>
      The partner evaluation runs as a midterm pulse and an end-of-term
      survey. Teams that do well keep their partner informed, including when
      the news is bad. Situational awareness, knowing where the project
      stands and who needs to hear it, may be the most important skill you
      take from capstone.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="Fall" title="When the class meets" wide>
  <TermSessions term="fall" />
  <Notes time="1:30" cue="Point out demo days in weeks 5 and 10.">
    <p>
      Drawn from the week-by-week schedule. Workshops are graded work done in
      class; demo days are all-hands, and presenting at one earns extra
      credit. A week with no session has no class unless an announcement says
      otherwise.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="The first three weeks" title="What's due, and what happens in class" wide>
  <TermSessions term="fall" from={0} to={2} rows={["Due", "In class"]} />
  <Notes time="1:30" cue="Use weeks 0 and 1 to read ahead.">
    <p>
      Also from the schedule. Teams are announced in week 1, and the
      introduction email goes out that week. Week 2 brings the first TA
      check-in, the Term Startup list, and the Team Charter. Use the time
      before your team forms to read the assignment pages.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="This term" title="This year's details are in announcements">
  <ul>
    <li>Your team's TA, and how to reach them</li>
    <li>Rooms, office hours, and meeting times</li>
    <li>The exact due date and time of each assignment</li>
    <li>The multidisciplinary projects, and any project that may split into two teams</li>
  </ul>
  <Notes time="1:00">
    <p>
      The handbook is reused every year, so it names terms and weeks only.
      Anything tied to this year's calendar or this year's people is in
      announcements. Every team has a TA, with a standing check-in in the
      weeks the schedule marks.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="Resume and Intent" title="Think beyond OSU">
  <ul>
    <li>Your project is something you'll put on your resume: pick one you'd want to talk about</li>
    <li class="fragment">You meet an instructor about your resume this term; check announcements for the sign-up</li>
  </ul>
  <Notes time="1:00">
    <p>
      The assignment itself carries the details. The resume meeting is in
      person; the sign-up link and the room are in announcements.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="Choosing a project" title="Choose on interest">
  <ul>
    <li>The range of projects is wide: read past the first few</li>
    <li class="fragment">Know yourself: what you want to learn, and how you like to work</li>
    <li class="fragment">Expect to meet new people; they're your future colleagues</li>
    <li class="fragment">Joining an ECE or multidisciplinary (ENGR) team means enrolling in that section instead</li>
  </ul>
  <Notes time="1:30" cue="Name this year's multidisciplinary projects only if the announcement is out.">
    <p>
      This year's multidisciplinary list, and who to talk to about it, is in
      announcements. Some project descriptions invite questions to the
      partner; asking doesn't reserve a spot.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<QrSlide kicker="Projects" title="Find the projects you like" url={portal.home}>
  <p>
    Sign in with your ONID and set your interests in your profile, and the
    list is ordered by them.
  </p>
  <Notes time="1:00" cue="Leave this up until most phones are down.">
    <p>The project list can change until the first lecture.</p>
  </Notes>
</QrSlide>

<Slide kicker="Bidding" title="How you get your project and team">
  <ul>
    <li>Rank six projects in the Bidding Survey, by the end of this week</li>
    <li class="fragment">The instructors place every student, balancing skills and preferences across teams</li>
    <li class="fragment">Teams are 3 to 4 students in the usual case</li>
    <li class="fragment">Friends: rank the same projects in the same order; it helps, and guarantees nothing</li>
    <li class="fragment">Proposed your own project? Rank it first, then five others</li>
    <li class="fragment">Teams are announced in class in week 1</li>
  </ul>
  <Notes time="2:30" cue="Take questions on bidding here.">
    <p>
      From For Students and the Bidding Survey page. Placement does its best
      for a top choice and can't guarantee one. Teams run from 2 to 5
      depending on enrollment and what the project needs. The survey link
      arrives by email from Qualtrics; a survey not submitted in time means
      you're placed without your input.
    </p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<QrSlide kicker="The handbook" title="Everything else is in the handbook" url={handbook}>
  <p>
    Every assignment page says what is due, when, and how it is scored.
  </p>
  <Notes time="1:00">
    <p>The schedule and the Assignments Overview are the two pages to bookmark.</p>
  </Notes>
</QrSlide>

<Slide kicker="This week" title="Before teams form" wide>
  <TermSessions term="fall" from={0} to={0} rows={["Due", "Read"]} />
  <Notes time="1:00" cue="End here and open the floor.">
    <p>From the schedule's week 0. The Bidding Survey closes when teams are formed, so it has no late window.</p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<Slide kicker="Sources" title="Where this deck comes from">
  <ul class="deck-small">
    <li>Grade and AI policy: Assignments Overview</li>
    <li>Class sessions and due weeks: Schedule</li>
    <li>Bidding and team formation: For Students, and the Bidding Survey</li>
    <li>The outcome ladder: the Shipping guide</li>
    <li>Nine hours a week: For Partners</li>
  </ul>
  <Notes time="0:30">
    <p>Questions until the end of the session.</p>
  </Notes>
</Slide>

<style>
  /* A closing line under a list, set apart from its last item. */
  .after-list {
    margin-block-start: 56px;
  }
</style>
